From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:17:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL=g53 on every prompt
 window, 1 row and up, so a prompt row's bits never depend on its window
 (glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc)

With TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL=g53, only prompt chunk windows of 65 rows or more took glm53f-afd's expert kernels (expert
prompt kernels from glm53f-afd, written by Hugh Madden (@dangerm00se, github.com/hughmadden), glm53f-afd @ 91db3cc);
windows of 1-64 rows stayed on TensorFold's kernels, whose bits differ. A prompt row's window depends on how the
attention node fills the prompt: alone or grouped with other prompts (TF_GLM_MULTI_PREFILL), whole or split at a kept
point of the 64-row prompt grid (a drafted prompt keeps its state there), a cache resume's tail, a prefill pair's
last chunk. So the same row got glm53f-afd's bits one way and TensorFold's the other, and replies depended on how
they were served: a 70-row prompt whose drafted and plain runs differed fills as 64 + 6 rows drafted (every row on
TensorFold's kernels) and as one 70-row window plain (every row on glm53f-afd's).

Now every prompt chunk window of the expert node (MODE_CHUNK, MODE_SLICE, MODE_PAIR and MODE_LANES forwards), of any
size from 1 row to the node's prompt rows, runs through the large-M kernels at the configuration they run up to 2,048
rows (32-row groups, one K split each, BF16 SwiGLU). glm53f-rank's own header states that with the same K splits and
SwiGLU a row gets the same bits whatever else is in the batch, the rows per group, the tilings and the row count
included; its default runs the split kernels (sk 8, skd 2: other bits) below 65 rows, which the port does not use.
So a prompt row's bits are the same in a window of 1 row as of 4,096. Decode and verify windows (MODE_EAGER) stay on
TensorFold's decode kernels as before.

- families/glm5_next/cuda/g53_experts.py: default_cfg takes 1..4,096 rows (the up-to-2,048-row setting below 65
  too); TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL_ROWS defaults to 1 (1..4,096; a higher floor is for measurements, and the node's startup
  line then says a prompt row's bits depend on its window); Node.compute writes the reply rows it is given (the
  link's own under TF_GLM_MCDMA_INFLIGHT, whose reply then goes out on the link's second stream as TensorFold's
  kernels' does) or the node's own as before.
- families/glm5_next/cuda/experts_node.py: compute passes its ``out`` to the g53 hook.
- THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md: the glm53f-afd section states the window rule.
No CUDA source changed (g53_rank.cu, .cpp and .h as before; the binding already takes 1..4,096 rows), so the
prebuilt extension tensorfold_glm_g53_v1 is unchanged. TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL unset (the default) is every call as
before.

Tests: tests/test_glm_g53_windows.py (new, CPU): the AFD attention node at world 1 on the fake model with the 64-row
prompt grid, every prompt window rank 0 fills recorded against the wire's chunk forwards; four 40-row prompts alone
and grouped, a 70-row prompt drafted (64 + 6), resumed from its kept 64 rows (a 6-row tail) and plain (70), a
600-row prompt in prefill pairs drafted and plain: under the old 65-row floor some row of each pair of scenarios takes
glm53f-afd's kernels one way and TensorFold's the other; under the default every prompt row of every scenario takes
glm53f-afd's, and no decode window does. tests/test_glm_g53_kernel.py (CPU): the plan and its bound at 1-4,096 rows,
the configuration below 65 rows, every window's bit fields (sk 1, skd 1, BF16 SwiGLU at 1..4,096 rows), the switch's
new default and range, the dispatch of 1-row windows, the hook's output buffer. tests/cuda/test_glm_g53_kernel.py
(GPU): byte equality with glm53f-rank's own large-M kernels at 1-4,096 rows (1, 2, 7, 16, 33 and 64 rows added), FP32
SwiGLU and the intermediates at small windows too, a row's bits in every window size 1..64 and larger ones against
one 4,096-row call, the schedules a small window could take keep every bit, faults at 1 row, the CPU reference at 1
row, the node's dispatch of 1-row windows into the link's reply rows, and times at 1-64 rows beside the EXL3 prompt
kernel's (TF_GLM_EXL3_PROMPT=1).

Co-authored-by: MiaAI-Lab <MiaAI-Lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

